      {{- /* Per user request 2026-06-30: the search header is
           structured in three parts:

           (1) The data-search-header-default attribute is
               ALWAYS the no-search format ("Media (89 -
               Showing 1-60)" or similar). The JS uses this
               when the visitor clears the search input (via
               Reset or by deleting all chars).

           (2) The visible text is one of two formats:
               - No search: "Media (89 - Showing 1-60)"
               - Server search (?q=...): "Media (89 - search
                 'st' - showing 33 of 33)" — NO <em>This page</em>

           (3) The hidden spans are ALWAYS present (regardless
               of whether there's a search query):
               - data-search-header-n: the per-page limit
                 (e.g. 60). Used by the JS as the N in the
                 "search showing M of N <em>This page</em>"
                 format.
               - data-search-header-total: the directory total
                 (e.g. 89). Used by the JS as the directory total
                 in the search format.
               - data-search-header-current: the current visible
                 text (captures the server-rendered format
                 including whether <em>This page</em> was
                 rendered). Used by the JS to know what format
                 was last rendered.

               Previously the hidden spans were only in the
               no-search branch, so the JS read them as 0
               when the visitor typed in the input on a
               form-submitted page, producing wrong values
               like "Media (0 - search 'str' - showing 33 of 0)".
      */ -}}
      {{- /* Per user request 2026-06-30: data-search-header-default is ALWAYS
           the no-search format (the "reset" state for the JS to restore to
           when the visitor clears the search). Hidden spans n/total/current
           are ALWAYS present so the JS can read them regardless of whether
           there's a server-rendered search. The visible text is either
           the no-search format (when input is empty) or the server-search
           format (when ?q= is set, no <em>This page</em>).

           The JS determines whether to render <em>This page</em> by comparing
           the ?q= URL param with the input.value — if they match, the page is
